id: rust.borrow-lifetimes
kind: rust
title: Borrowing and lifetimes
aliases: lifetimes, borrow checker, lifetime elision
tags: ownership, fundamentals
questions:
  - when do you need to write a lifetime annotation?
invariant: A lifetime names how long a borrow must stay valid; it constrains relationships, it never extends a value's life.
answer_30s: Lifetimes describe the relationship between borrows and the data they point at, and annotations are needed only when that relationship is ambiguous.
answer_2m: |
  Elision covers the common single-input cases, so annotations appear where the
  relationship is not obvious: several input references with one output, or a
  struct holding a reference. 'static means the whole program (literals, statics,
  boxed ownership), and in server code the practical rule is that long-lived
  structures and handlers should own their data (String, Vec, Arc), because a
  borrow cannot outlive the task that holds it. Reaching for 'static everywhere
  is a smell that holds clones; the honest fix is deciding who owns the data.
when_to_use:
  - structs holding references, functions with several reference arguments
avoid_when:
  - shared state across tasks or long-lived handlers (own the data instead)
minimal_example: |
  // Elision handles this: one input borrow determines the output borrow.
  fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str { s.split_whitespace().next().unwrap_or("") }

  // Needed: two inputs, so the relationship must be stated.
  fn longer<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str { if a.len() >= b.len() { a } else { b } }
failure_modes:
  - sprinkling 'static on a struct to make it compile, then cloning to fill it
  - returning a reference to a value created in the function
related:
  - rust.ownership-move
  - rust.arc
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch10-03-lifetime-syntax.html
